[description]

[b]GitLab[/b] wraps standard Git with a DevSecOps suite: identity, merge requests, CI/CD, container registry, package registry, security scanning, planning (issues, epics, milestones), wiki, snippets, and Pages. Your laptop still runs [code]git commit[/code], [code]git branch[/code], and [code]git merge[/code] as Oh My Git drills; GitLab stores bare repos server-side plus product metadata in PostgreSQL—not inside commit objects.

[b]Hierarchy:[/b] [i]groups[/i] nest [i]subgroups[/i] and [i]projects[/i]; a project typically hosts one primary Git repository (optional additional repos in some setups). Permissions inherit downward like a filesystem—powerful for enterprises, easy to misconfigure if admins ignore inherited roles.

[b]Merge requests:[/b] GitLab’s name for pull requests—branch, push, open MR, review, pipelines must pass, merge when policy allows. Underlying transport remains Git over SSH/HTTPS; MR IDs are forge records until merge writes refs.

[b]GitLab.com vs self-managed:[/b] SaaS on gitlab.com trades control for speed; self-managed installs in your VPC or airgap with your LDAP/SAML, object storage, and runners. Remote URL format stays familiar; upgrade and backup responsibility shifts with deployment model.

[b]CI/CD first-class:[/b] [code].gitlab-ci.yml[/code] at repo root (or includes) defines pipelines triggered by push, MR, schedule, API. Runners poll for jobs—instance, group, or project tokens on your hardware. Different YAML shape than GitHub Actions; scripting skills transfer.

[b]Registry and packages:[/b] container images and language packages live beside Git with permission inheritance—pipelines publish artifacts consumers pull using CI job tokens scoped narrowly.

[b]Why teams pick GitLab:[/b] single vendor for SCM plus CI plus registry plus baseline security scans; strong self-managed story; sometimes cost or data residency. Trade-off: heavier footprint than ultra-light forges and vocabulary learning curve for newcomers from GitHub.

[b]Epics and planning layer:[/b] issues roll into epics and milestones above raw Git—MRs reference issues with [code]Closes[/code] keywords. Planning metadata does not travel if you only [code]git clone[/code] bare repos elsewhere; export issues when mirroring for disaster recovery.

[b]Pages and snippets:[/b] static sites from Git branches and shared paste snippets live beside repos—permissions inherit from project visibility; do not publish secrets in snippet URLs or Pages build logs.

[b]When things go wrong:[/b] clone works but push fails on permission—check group inheritance vs guest role. MR visible but pipeline missing—runners offline or tags mismatch [code]tags: docker[/code]. Confusing duplicate project URLs after group moves means stale remotes—update [code]git remote -v[/code]. Container registry 403 during CI often means job token lacks read_project on dependency image project—increase token scope carefully.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] learn GitLab’s objects (groups, projects, MRs, pipelines) the way you learned Git’s objects—then CLI and API clicks stay predictable.
